<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id9" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">Xian Jiaotong University</span>, <span id="id10" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_country">China</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Autoregressive Large Language Models (AR-LLMs) are widely used in software engineering (SE) but face limitations in processing code structure information and suffer from high inference latency. Diffusion LLMs (DLLMs) offer a promising alternative with global bidirectional encoding and decoupled generation steps.
This work presents the first comprehensive evaluation of DLLMs across the software development lifecycle, including code generation, defect detection, and program repair.
On a large-scale benchmark of 52,937 tasks, 7B-parameter DLLMs outperform AR-LLMs with a 30% average accuracy improvement, achieving a 113% gain on cross-file repair, while maintaining superior efficiency and reduced latency. Our results establish DLLMs as a superior paradigm for SE tasks.
The code is publicly available at <a href="https://anonymous.4open.science/r/all_datasets-B4A6" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://anonymous.4open.science/r/all_datasets-B4A6</a></p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) have been deeply integrated into the entire software development lifecycle (SDLC) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. From commercial tools such as GitHub Copilot <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and Amazon CodeWhisperer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Traub et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to open-weight models like Code Llama <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roziere et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and CodeT5+ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, LLMs are now indispensable in software engineering (SE) tasks, including daily maintenance, bug fixing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, test generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schwarting et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and code review <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tufano et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. These tools are expected to effectively comprehend program execution logic and generate actionable code tailored to specific contexts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, over the past few years, traditional LLMs have underperformed on SE tasks precisely because they are built upon an autoregressive (AR) architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>)</cite>: each prediction requires a full forward pass over all preceding context before predicting a single token.
Concretely, AR-LLMs decode strictly from left to right. AR-LLMs cannot simultaneously observe long-range or hierarchical structural relationships, making it difficult to capture structural information, resulting in weaker capabilities when processing long texts with multiple logical structures. This limitation stems from the autoregressive architecture itself and hinders further advancement of AR-LLMs in SE applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite>.
Moreover, AR-LLMs generate tokens in a strictly autoregressive fashion.
Consequently, the number of forward executions (i.e., steps) is linearly coupled to the output length <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, and the next token cannot be predicted until the previous one has been completed. This sequential dependency introduces substantial latency on consumer-grade hardware <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Savinov et al., 2021b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To overcome these limitations, a new paradigm is urgently needed. Recently, a new class of LLMs <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diffusion LLMs (DLLMs)</span> has emerged and gained traction in general-purpose domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. This models adopt a <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">global parallel denoising</span> mechanism, which has been validated for both effectiveness and efficiency. The current DLLMs has demonstrated performance comparable to the most advanced AR-LLMs in most fields <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">DLLMs offer two key advantages over AR-LLMs: (1) <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Global Bidirectional Encoding:</span> Unlike AR models, DLLMs are not constrained by left-to-right generation. They can flexibly select and update token positions during each denoising step. This allows DLLMs to prioritize contextually relevant information and assign higher weights to it, thereby reducing information loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
(2) <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step-Length Decoupling:</span> In DLLMs, the number of tokens to be generated (i.e., length) and the number of denoising iterations (i.e., steps) are decoupled: the latter is fixed a priori, whereas the former scales with the target sequence, so a longer sequence does not demand additional steps, in contrast to autoregressive models where length and steps are necessarily equal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">These advantages align naturally with the requirements of SE tasks such as real-time defect detection, error reporting, and cross-file bug fixing, which demand both global context awareness and low-latency interaction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
Despite these theoretical advantages, existing research has primarily focused on evaluating DLLMs in code generation tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Author, s</a>)</cite>, leaving a systematic assessment of their capabilities across the entire SDLC largely unexplored.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">first introduce DLLMs into the full SDLC</span>, including code generation, defect detection, program repair, and cross-file maintenance. We conduct a large-scale empirical evaluation across six benchmarks—Defects4J <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Just et al., 2014</a>)</cite>, Devign <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, SWE-bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jimenez et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, Bears <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madeiral et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, Mercury <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and HumanEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021</a>)</cite>—comprising a total of <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">52,937 tasks</span>. Our results show that, at the similar parameter scale (7B), DLLMs outperform AR-LLMs in terms of both accuracy and efficiency. Specifically, DLLMs achieve an average improvement of <span id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">30%</span> across the six benchmarks, with a <span id="S1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">113%</span> improvement on SWE-bench for cross-file repair. Moreover, DLLMs exhibit superior global consistency under reduced decoding latency, making them a promising alternative to AR-LLMs in real-world SE applications.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.0.1. </span>Autoregressive Large Language Models</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Autoregressive generation is the predominant paradigm in large language models, commonly known as AR-LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Both reading and generation in AR-LLMs are linear processes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS0.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reading Process:</span> During the reading phase, AR-LLMs employ a token-by-token causal masked scanning mechanism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite>. For any position <math id="S2.SS0.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, the attention weights are hard-coded as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{\text{causal}}(i,j)=\begin{cases}-\infty,&amp;j&gt;i,\\
0,&amp;j\leq i,\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mtext>causal</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{\text{causal}}(i,j)=\begin{cases}-\infty,&amp;j&gt;i,\\
0,&amp;j\leq i,\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{\text{causal}}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mtext>causal</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{\text{causal}}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> defines a causal mask ensuring that the model only considers tokens up to the current position <math id="S2.SS0.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and ignores future tokens. Specifically, for each position <math id="S2.SS0.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in the sequence, the model can only attend to tokens at positions <math id="S2.SS0.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leq i</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning token <math id="S2.SS0.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> can only read the generated sequence <math id="S2.SS0.SSS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.0.2. </span>Generation Process:</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the generation phase, AR-LLMs produce tokens one-by-one from left to right, expanding the generation scope token-by-token. The generation follows the chain rule <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2018</a>)</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{x})=\prod_{t=1}^{T}p(x_{t}\mid x_{&lt;t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{x})=\prod_{t=1}^{T}p(x_{t}\mid x_{&lt;t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the total length of the sequence, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th element in the sequence, and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{&lt;t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{&lt;t}</annotation></semantics></math> represents all elements preceding <math id="S2.SS0.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="S2.SS0.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>). The product symbol <math id="S2.SS0.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∏</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that the joint probability of the sequence is the product of the conditional probabilities of each element. Specifically, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x_{t}\mid x_{&lt;t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x_{t}\mid x_{&lt;t})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the conditional probability of the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th element given all previous elements. This formulation ensures that each element is generated based solely on its preceding context, maintaining the causality of the generation process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.0.3. </span>Generation Rate:</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since AR-LLMs update only a single token representation in each forward pass, this results in linear time complexity <math id="S2.SS0.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T)</annotation></semantics></math> and linear FLOPs accumulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tay et al., 2022</a>)</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FLOPs}_{\text{AR}}(T)=T\cdot C_{\text{forward}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>FLOPs</mtext><mtext>AR</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>forward</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FLOPs}_{\text{AR}}(T)=T\cdot C_{\text{forward}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FLOPs}_{\text{AR}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>FLOPs</mtext><mtext>AR</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FLOPs}_{\text{AR}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the total number of floating-point operations required to process a sequence of length <math id="S2.SS0.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{forward}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>forward</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{forward}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of FLOPs required for a single forward pass. On consumer GPUs, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{forward}}\approx 10^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>forward</mtext></msub><mo>≈</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{forward}}\approx 10^{10}</annotation></semantics></math> FLOPs. When <math id="S2.SS0.SSS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T&gt;512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T&gt;512</annotation></semantics></math>, the wall-clock latency exceeds 400 ms, which may exceed the real-time requirements of certain applications.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Diffusion Language Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">DLLMs have recently emerged as a focal point of AI research, offering a novel paradigm that departs markedly from autoregressive LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song and Ermon, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rombach et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.1. </span>Model Architecture</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The core of a Diffusion language model lies in a learnable reverse-denoising process that implements global bidirectional encoding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015</a>)</cite>.
The denoising network reverses a Markov chain in which <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>)</cite>:
(i) <em id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">forward diffusion</em> progressively injects noise into clean data until it becomes pure noise <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015</a>)</cite>;
(ii) <em id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">learnable reverse denoising</em> starts from pure noise and iteratively removes noise to recover the original data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song and Ermon, 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike AR-LLMs, DLLMs remove the causal attention mask, enabling global bidirectional context <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
In a single iteration the model updates <em id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all</em> tokens simultaneously, eliminating the sequential, word-by-word dependency of autoregressive generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2017</a>)</cite>.
Because generation is a “global refinement” rather than a “token-by-token construction,” the number of refinement iterations (steps) can be set independently of the sentence length, achieving step-length decoupling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.2. </span>Training Process</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The training process of DLLMs is a noising process (also known as a diffusion process), the objective of which is to train the model to restore the original, clean text from a noised version <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
This noising process is defined by the following formula:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(y_{i}^{t}|y_{i}^{0})=\begin{cases}1-t&amp;\text{if }y_{i}^{t}=y_{i}^{0}\\
t&amp;\text{if }y_{i}^{t}=\text{[MASK]}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>t</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>[MASK]</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(y_{i}^{t}|y_{i}^{0})=\begin{cases}1-t&amp;\text{if }y_{i}^{t}=y_{i}^{0}\\
t&amp;\text{if }y_{i}^{t}=\text{[MASK]}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this formula, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(y_{i}^{t}|y_{i}^{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(y_{i}^{t}|y_{i}^{0})</annotation></semantics></math> represents the conditional probability that an original clean token <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from sequence <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes state <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at a given time step <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (a continuous noise schedule from 0 to 1), where <span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[MASK]</span> is the special token the model must restore. The training objective of the model is to learn to accurately reverse this noising process by minimizing a cross-entropy loss function, computed only on the masked positions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Savinov et al., 2021a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.3. </span>Generation Process</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At inference time the user feeds an SE task description (e.g., a functional specification for code generation or a GitHub issue for bug repair) into the trained DLLM.
The model outputs an immediately usable solution—executable code, a vulnerability classification, or a patch that passes all tests—after <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> fixed denoising steps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Study Design</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Overview and Research Questions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present the first systematic evaluation of DLLMs across the complete SDLC: code generation, defect detection, program repair, and cross-file maintenance.
Motivated by their global bidirectional encoding and step-length decoupling, which promise higher context fidelity and lower latency, we inject DLLMs into real-world SE tasks to verify whether they maintain or improve accuracy while significantly accelerating inference.
We formulate two research questions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1. Do DLLMs achieve higher effectiveness than AR-LLMs on SE tasks?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2. Do DLLMs achieve superior inference speed than AR-LLMs on SE tasks?</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Benchmark Selection</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To answer RQ1 and RQ2 we construct a comprehensive benchmark suite that covers the entire SDLC. We select six widely-adopted open-source datasets spanning function-, file-, and repository-level issues, and encompassing syntactic, logical, and cross-file semantic faults.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1. </span>Code Generation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(1) <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HumanEval</span> (164 cases): Function-level code generation from natural language descriptions to executable code <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
(2) <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mercury</span> (1,889 cases): Algorithmic problems focused on testing the logical correctness of code <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2. </span>Defect Detection</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(1) <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Devign</span> (27,318 cases): Function-level vulnerability detection (binary classification: present/absent) to assess the model’s understanding of defect semantics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.
(2) <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bears (Detection)</span> (251 cases): Multi-file, multi-type vulnerability detection requiring the model to accurately classify defect categories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madeiral et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3. </span>Program Repair</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(1) <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Defects4J</span> (835 cases): Repair of classic fine-grained Java defects within a single version and single trigger test <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Just et al., 2014</a>)</cite>.
(2) <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bears (Repair)</span> (251 cases): Multi-file program repair for Travis CI failures across builds and modules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madeiral et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.4. </span>Cross-File Maintenance</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(1) <span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SWE-bench</span> (2,294 cases): Utilizing real GitHub issues, requiring the model to locate and repair cross-file defects in the full repository context <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jimenez et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt seven metrics that are consistent with our research questions and are widely used in prior studies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pass@K</span>. For code generation tasks, we evaluate functional correctness using Pass@K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. This metric calculates the percentage of problems for which at least one of K generated solutions passes all provided test cases. We report Pass@1 and Pass@10.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DDF1</span>. For defect detection on the <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Devign</span> and <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bears (Detection)</span> benchmarks, we use the DDF1 score. DDF1 is the harmonic mean of Precision and Recall, providing a balanced measure of bug identification accuracy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Wang2021devign</span>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PR</span>. For program repair on <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Defects4J</span> and <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bears (Repair)</span>, we use the Program Repair (PR) rate. PR is the percentage of defects correctly fixed by the first generated patch that compiles and passes all tests <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">Goues2019automated</span>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MDVR</span>. For the <span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SWE-bench</span> benchmark, we use the Multi-file Detection Success Rate (MDVR). This metric measures the success rate of identifying all necessary code locations for a fix in a multi-file program <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jimenez et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PRR</span>. Also for <span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SWE-bench</span>, we use the Patch Resolution Rate (PRR). PRR is the percentage of generated patches that correctly solve an issue by compiling and passing all tests <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jimenez et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TPS</span>. To assess time efficiency, we report Tokens per second (TPS). This metric measures generation throughput, calculated as the average number of output tokens generated per second.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T-&lt;avg&gt;</span>. We also measure time efficiency with average execution time (T-&lt;avg&gt;). This metric reports the average time in seconds to complete one benchmark case, representing overall latency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Implementation Details</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To systematically answer the two research questions, we select two state-of-the-art <em id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">commercial</em> large language models—one Diffusion (<em id="S3.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mercury-Diffusion 7B</em>) and one autoregressive (<em id="S3.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Llama</em>)—as representative DLLM and AR-LLM, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Study Results</h2>

